items: ci-status-guard-races-the-shared-repo-index-lock coord-status-activity-lookup-reads-the-callers-cwd
paths: file:tests/e2e/ci-status-guard.sh file:tests/BUGS.md file:tests/SPRINT.md file:scripts/coord-status file:scripts/BUGS.md
scope: |
  `tests/e2e/ci-status-guard.sh` builds its claim fixture with `git -C "$ROOT" worktree add`, i.e.
  it mutates the repository every other agent shares, from inside the pre-push suite. Moved into a
  throwaway `--local --no-checkout` clone (0.066 s), with the tree's `scripts/` copied over the
  clone's committed copy so the code under test stays the WORKING TREE's, and an assertion at both
  ends that the shared repo's `claim-wt*` registrations and `feature/ci-red-main-*` branches are
  unchanged. Measured on complete runs: 67 s -> 33 s, because `coord-status` walks
  `git worktree list` and this host carried 110 registrations (94 after the dead ones were swept).

  WIDENED 2026-08-13, before the edit and not after. The clone made a LATENT defect in
  `scripts/coord-status` block: `claim_activity_epoch` is the one function in that file whose three
  `git log` calls carry no `-C "$ROOT"`, so the commit-activity rule resolves against the CALLER'S
  working directory, and its last call hardcodes `origin/main` instead of `$REMOTE_REF`. Measured
  on the live repo at one instant: 4 claims reported stale from inside the repo, 5 from `/tmp` —
  the same script, the same claims, a different answer, and a claim reported stale is one the
  triage table says may be released and reclaimed. Fix is `-C "$ROOT"` on all three plus
  `$REMOTE_REF`; the guard's commit-activity case is what proves it, since that case now depends on
  a branch that exists only in the clone.
next: final smoke running on the rebased branch; then mark the SPRINT row [x], push, release
